WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:05.840
There are two types of people in this world—those
who can count, and those who can’t.

00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:11.040
And I’d argue there are also two types of
fiction writers—those who take a backseat

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:16.480
and let their work take the spotlight, and
those who are as iconic as their work, sometimes

00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:17.360
even more so.

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:23.600
Whether deliberately crafted or not, these
writers have a legacy that is so monumental,

00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:27.360
it’s an uphill battle to read their work
on its own terms.

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:30.000
But maybe there’s a third type.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:35.840
The type of writer whose complex persona is
so intertwined with his fiction (or hers)

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:37.520
that to ignore

00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:39.600
him is to ignore his work entirely.

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:43.840
Folks, I’m talking about Kurt Vonnegut,
Jr.

00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:50.080
Writer of such genre-bending satirical novels
like The Sirens of Titan, Cat’s Cradle,

00:00:50.080 --> 00:00:56.160
and Slaughterhouse-Five, a novel which regularly
pops up on “Best Novels of All Time” lists,

00:00:56.160 --> 00:01:00.320
Kurt Vonnegut practically moonlighted as his
own Public Relations rep.

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:06.160
In fact, he was so successful at crafting
his persona, he spent his later years waffling

00:01:06.160 --> 00:01:12.320
back and forth between fighting against his
reputation and actively leaning into it.

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:16.320
In other words, Kurt Vonnegut was a man of
contradictions.

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:22.000
A man who wanted it all and got it, only to
be unsure it’s what he actually wanted in

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:23.360
the first place.

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:29.120
What resulted from his ongoing struggles with
his identity and reputation is a mish-mash

00:01:29.120 --> 00:01:36.400
of outlandish science-fiction, wacky characters,
biting satire, sobering reflections on our

00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:39.760
place in the universe, and somber tragedy.

00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:45.120
And at the center of this glorious mess is
Kurt Vonnegut, Jr. himself, a man who would

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:51.040
change the face of modern science-fiction
and give new meaning to the term “metafiction.”

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:56.320
To quote a phrase that pops up time and again
in Kurt’s writing: “so it goes…”

00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:06.640
I’m going to begin my Kurt Vonnegut spiel
by doing something no one has ever done before

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:09.520
open with a dictionary quote.

00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:11.680
A truly revolutionary idea.

00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:17.440
According to University of Minnesota English
Professor Peter J. Reed in the Dictionary

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:18.960
of Literary Biography:

00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:24.960
“The contribution of Kurt Vonnegut Jr. to
American literature is twofold: through his

00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:30.400
artistry (and persistence) he has helped to
elevate the pulp genre of science fiction

00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:35.280
to the level of critical recognition; and
through his philosophy he offers a mixture

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:41.040
of wistful humanism and cynical existentialism
that implies a way of dealing with modern

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:45.360
realities completely different from that of
most American writers.”

00:02:46.640 --> 00:02:50.800
This entry about Vonnegut is a helpful jumping-off
point for two reasons:

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:51.600
1.

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:56.640
It encapsulates the fundamental contradictions
of Vonnegut’s outlook on life

00:02:56.640 --> 00:02:57.360
and 2.

00:02:57.360 --> 00:03:02.800
It emphasizes that his impact on science-fiction
literature is just as notable as the road

00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:06.240
that got him there—most notably his persistence.

00:03:06.880 --> 00:03:12.960
When Vonnegut was born on November 11, 1922,
he was entering a world that was practically

00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:15.840
designed to shape a conflicted young man.

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:21.040
You see, November 11th was Armistice Day,
which we now know as Veterans Day.

00:03:21.040 --> 00:03:25.440
It was a day to commemorate the end of World
War I, which at that time was called

00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:30.080
“The Great War” because we had not yet
seen the more horrific sequel:

00:03:30.080 --> 00:03:31.840
World War II: Hitler Boogaloo

00:03:32.720 --> 00:03:38.480
Later in life, as Vonnegut’s identity became
inextricably linked with war, he was proud

00:03:38.480 --> 00:03:40.960
that he was born on a day associated with
peace.

00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:45.360
But even though the fighting was technically
over, there was still plenty of hostility

00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:50.400
against Germans to go around, and the Vonneguts
were very German.

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:53.280
Vonnegut even dubbed himself a “purebred
kraut.”

00:03:53.920 --> 00:03:56.000
The youngest of three children, Vonnegut enjoyed

00:03:56.000 --> 00:03:59.680
an upper-middle class upbringing in Indianapolis,
Indiana.

00:03:59.680 --> 00:04:04.240
But even the Vonneguts’ cushy lifestyle
couldn’t shield them from the Great Depression.

00:04:04.240 --> 00:04:08.480
But throughout familial ups-and-downs like
his family’s bankruptcy and Vonnegut’s

00:04:08.480 --> 00:04:13.120
inability to live up to his older brother’s
relentless superiority.

00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:14.320
Bernard was a whiz

00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:15.520
at science and well…

00:04:15.520 --> 00:04:21.920
Vonnegut was more of an artsy kid, there
is one event that overshadows everything.

00:04:21.920 --> 00:04:27.360
And it’s something that would not only radically
affect Vonnegut’s life but would kickstart

00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:32.960
Vonnegut’s inclination of facing traumatic
life experiences head-on in his writing.

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:36.160
That event was his mother’s suicide.

00:04:36.160 --> 00:04:43.360
And content warning for that, because we’re
about to go into some detail.

00:04:44.560 --> 00:04:49.760
At the age of twenty-one, Vonnegut had returned
home one weekend from military training when

00:04:49.760 --> 00:04:53.120
his sister Alice woke him up early Sunday
morning.

00:04:53.120 --> 00:04:54.640
And not just any Sunday morning.

00:04:55.360 --> 00:04:55.840
Mother’s Day.

00:04:56.720 --> 00:05:01.120
At the age of fifty-five, Vonnegut’s mother
Edith overdosed on sleeping pills.

00:05:01.680 --> 00:05:06.720
She left no note, so it’s unclear why Edith
took her own life, but what is certainly clear

00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:09.840
is t he profound effect it had on Vonnegut
and his family.

00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:16.000
Such is the secretive, put-a-happy-face-on-it
nature of Midwestern life that her suicide

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:18.480
wasn’t even openly acknowledged as such.

00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:22.640
The coroner ruled it an accident, and even
though Vonnegut’s father would sometimes

00:05:22.640 --> 00:05:26.880
admit the truth to relatives, he never did
so with his children.

00:05:26.880 --> 00:05:30.800
Left with the tragic loss of his mother, coupled
with everyone’s refusal to admit the truth

00:05:30.800 --> 00:05:35.920
about her death, Vonnegut didn’t run from
it—he openly grappled with it.

00:05:35.920 --> 00:05:41.040
In social circles, he would steer conversations
toward suicide, and the topic would crop up

00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:43.040
over and over in his work.

00:05:43.680 --> 00:05:49.200
And in true Kurt Vonnegut style, he didn’t
alter the truth that much, even in his fiction.

00:05:49.920 --> 00:05:54.640
In Breakfast of Champions, a zany whirlwind
of a novel, Vonnegut describes the suicide

00:05:54.640 --> 00:06:00.080
of Celia Hoover, the wife of one of the two
main characters, Dwayne Hoover, in gruesome

00:06:00.080 --> 00:06:01.440
yet detached detail.

00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:08.720
Having just drunk a bunch of Drāno, "Celia
became a small volcano, since she was composed

00:06:08.720 --> 00:06:12.400
of the same sorts of substances which commonly
clogged drains."

00:06:13.200 --> 00:06:14.320
Vonnegut himself—ahem,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.960 --> 00:06:19.840
I mean, “the narrator”—notes,
“. . . both our mothers committed suicide.

00:06:20.400 --> 00:06:22.240
Bunny’s mother ate Drano.

00:06:22.240 --> 00:06:26.000
My mother ate sleeping pills, which wasn’t
nearly as horrible.”

00:06:26.720 --> 00:06:30.960
And in an earlier novel, God Bless You, Mr.
Rosewater, Vonnegut writes:

00:06:31.920 --> 00:06:34.560
“Sons of suicides seldom do well.

00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:37.520
Characteristically, they find life lacking
a certain zing.

00:06:38.080 --> 00:06:43.440
They tend to feel more rootless than most,
even in a notoriously rootless nation.

00:06:43.440 --> 00:06:48.400
They are squeamishly incurious about the past
and numbly certain about the future to this

00:06:48.400 --> 00:06:53.600
grisly extent: they suspect that they, too,
will kill themselves.”

00:06:53.600 --> 00:06:56.880
I should just say right
now that Vonnegut doesn’t end up killing

00:06:56.880 --> 00:06:57.360
himself.

00:06:57.360 --> 00:07:00.800
He dies in 2007 at the ripe&nbsp;
old age of eighty-four.

00:07:01.360 --> 00:07:04.000
And not from the countless&nbsp;
Pall Malls he chain-smoked&nbsp;

00:07:04.000 --> 00:07:04.960
through the decades

00:07:04.960 --> 00:07:07.200
which he thought for sure would kill him.

00:07:07.760 --> 00:07:10.560
No, he died of brain injuries from a fall.

00:07:10.560 --> 00:07:11.360
Which is sad.

00:07:11.920 --> 00:07:15.920
But not the bleak fate he thought about for
himself.

00:07:15.920 --> 00:07:20.640
But this quote also sheds some light on perhaps
why so much of Vonnegut’s work is focused

00:07:20.640 --> 00:07:25.680
on the meaninglessness of existence and humanity’s
likely doomed future.

00:07:25.680 --> 00:07:29.440
His mother’s suicide also permeates his
work in other ways.

00:07:29.440 --> 00:07:35.440
Says Kurt Vonnegut biographer Charles J. Shields:
“...his mother’s suicide permanently erased

00:07:35.440 --> 00:07:37.600
some of life’s luster for him.”

00:07:37.600 --> 00:07:40.720
In many of Vonnegut’s
novels and short stories “his obsession

00:07:40.720 --> 00:07:44.880
with self-destruction edges his fiction like
a black border.”

00:07:44.880 --> 00:07:49.120
Vonnegut’s predilection to grapple with
his own past in his fiction

00:07:49.120 --> 00:07:54.400
by portraying his mother characters as “either
morbid, crazy, or suicidal.”

00:07:55.120 --> 00:08:00.480
Just seven months after the death of his mother,
the second of Vonnegut’s two major life-altering

00:08:00.480 --> 00:08:02.160
events was set in motion—War.

00:08:03.200 --> 00:08:08.480
In December 1944, the Germans launched their
final offensive with the Battle of the Bulge,

00:08:08.480 --> 00:08:13.360
and after his division was swept into the
chaos, Vonnegut was one of seven thousand

00:08:13.360 --> 00:08:19.120
troops who was taken prisoner and marched
hundreds of miles into Germany, leaving his

00:08:19.120 --> 00:08:20.800
family uncertain of his fate.

00:08:21.360 --> 00:08:26.880
For six months, Vonnegut was listed “missing
in action,” and during this time, he, along

00:08:26.880 --> 00:08:31.040
with 150 other POWs, were taken to Dresden.

00:08:31.600 --> 00:08:35.840
And it was the ultimate “wrong place at
the wrong time” situation.

00:08:36.560 --> 00:08:43.280
On February 13 and 14, 1945, the British and
United States Air Forces firebombed the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.280 --> 00:08:44.960
ever-living-hell

00:08:44.960 --> 00:08:50.720
out of Dresden, resulting in so much carnage
that to this day the death tolls are unknown,

00:08:50.720 --> 00:08:56.480
potentially figuring somewhere between 35,000
and 135,000 people.

00:08:57.360 --> 00:09:02.560
For the next several months, Vonnegut and
the other POWs were forced into traumatizing,

00:09:02.560 --> 00:09:08.720
back-breaking work, gathering corpses for
massive funeral pyres and clearing rubble,

00:09:08.720 --> 00:09:12.160
all the while becoming increasingly sick and
malnourished.

00:09:12.880 --> 00:09:17.920
Then late that spring, Vonnegut and the remaining
POWs were marched to the Czech border and

00:09:17.920 --> 00:09:18.720
abandoned.

00:09:18.720 --> 00:09:20.560
But at last, they were free.

00:09:21.200 --> 00:09:25.840
So how in the heck does a man who endured
such tragedies in his life become a writer

00:09:25.840 --> 00:09:32.000
known for his wit, humor, and bizarre, off-kilter
views on life and man’s place in it?

00:09:32.000 --> 00:09:37.120
This is a writer, after all, who’s written
stories with titles like, “Guns Before Butter,”

00:09:37.760 --> 00:09:43.680
“FUBAR,” “Bagombo Snuff Box,” “Welcome
to the Monkey House,” and

00:09:44.320 --> 00:09:46.680
“The Big Space ****”

00:09:47.360 --> 00:09:49.200
—wait, this is PBS.

00:09:49.200 --> 00:09:52.720
I’m not allowed to say “Big Space ****,”
am I?—

00:09:54.960 --> 00:09:56.160
But here’s the thing.

00:09:56.160 --> 00:10:02.160
As out-there as Vonnegut’s stories can be
- and boy oh boy, can they be out-there - they

00:10:02.160 --> 00:10:05.760
are always firmly rooted in a strong moral
point-of-view.

00:10:05.760 --> 00:10:10.640
Fellow writer Dave Eggers says of Vonnegut’s
stories: “The satisfaction we draw from

00:10:10.640 --> 00:10:16.640
seeing some moral clarity, some linear order
brought to a knotted world, is impossible

00:10:16.640 --> 00:10:18.320
to overstate.”

00:10:18.320 --> 00:10:23.520
The moral compass in much of Vonnegut’s
work points in a progressive direction, leaning

00:10:23.520 --> 00:10:29.200
very heavily in favor of fairness to downtrodden
workers; equality for everyone, no matter

00:10:29.200 --> 00:10:34.640
their race, gender, or creed; and an overall
empathetic view of the poor, lowly, sick,

00:10:34.640 --> 00:10:35.760
and disadvantaged.

00:10:36.320 --> 00:10:41.440
In fact, in lectures and speeches, he would
oftentimes quote the socialist/activist Eugene

00:10:41.440 --> 00:10:46.880
V. Debs: “...while there is a lower class,
I am in it, and while there is a criminal

00:10:46.880 --> 00:10:52.000
element, I am of it, and while there is a
soul in prison, I am not free.”

00:10:52.960 --> 00:10:58.080
And then there is one of Vonnegut’s most
oft-quoted aphorisms: “God damn it, you’ve

00:10:58.080 --> 00:11:00.080
got to be kind.”

00:11:00.080 --> 00:11:05.680
Between Vonnegut’s repetition of such lofty
words to live by and his growing reputation

00:11:05.680 --> 00:11:11.120
as a writer in the turbulent 60s and 70s,
it’s no wonder then that he attracted the

00:11:11.120 --> 00:11:13.680
attention and allegiance of America’s youth.

00:11:13.680 --> 00:11:18.480
Disaffected by the never-ending war in Vietnam,
the seemingly relentless assassinations of

00:11:18.480 --> 00:11:23.440
iconic leaders, and a barrage of violence
across the country, many students turned to

00:11:23.440 --> 00:11:28.800
Vonnegut’s fiction as a way to both escape
from their harsh reality and stay tethered

00:11:28.800 --> 00:11:31.360
to it through the strong moral views in his work.

00:11:32.000 --> 00:11:36.720
So perhaps it’s no coincidence that Vonnegut’s
most iconic novel, Slaughterhouse-Five,

00:11:36.720 --> 00:11:40.560
was released at the turn of the decade in 1969

00:11:41.200 --> 00:11:43.360
a year many historians consider the end of

00:11:43.360 --> 00:11:48.720
the free-spirited “peace and love” sixties
and the beginning of a darker period in America.

00:11:49.600 --> 00:11:53.600
Slaughterhouse-Five marked the culmination
of Vonnegut’s struggles to grapple with

00:11:53.600 --> 00:11:56.720
his own war experience in his work.

00:11:56.720 --> 00:12:02.240
Rather than spice up his war novel with big,
exciting battles and macho war heroes, he

00:12:02.240 --> 00:12:08.160
once again leaned into the truth—the confusion
of war and the aimless powerlessness that

00:12:08.160 --> 00:12:09.840
so many soldiers like him felt.

00:12:10.720 --> 00:12:15.600
Slaughterhouse-Five also continued Vonnegut’s
trend of directly inserting himself in his

00:12:15.600 --> 00:12:16.800
fictional work

00:12:16.800 --> 00:12:21.760
beginning the novel with this
sentence: “All this happened, more or less.”

00:12:22.720 --> 00:12:24.000
More or less.

00:12:24.000 --> 00:12:24.800
Wink, wink.

00:12:25.600 --> 00:12:31.200
Vonnegut was continuing to fulfill his dream of&nbsp;
wanting to be a character in all of his works.

00:12:31.200 --> 00:12:36.240
But now that he was becoming a household name,
he wanted to have it both ways.

00:12:36.240 --> 00:12:41.600
He noted that to his fans he was “valuable
to them as a personality” while simultaneously

00:12:41.600 --> 00:12:44.560
finding this “disturbing in a way.”

00:12:44.560 --> 00:12:48.160
As Vonnegut enjoyed the fruits of being a
famous, well-known author

00:12:48.160 --> 00:12:50.800
fancy dinners, hobnobbing with celebrities,

00:12:50.800 --> 00:12:54.720
speaking at universities and&nbsp;
lecture halls for tidy fees

00:12:54.720 --> 00:12:57.200
he was slowly realizing that the image of himself

00:12:57.200 --> 00:12:59.920
that he had relentlessly presented in his work

00:12:59.920 --> 00:13:04.320
was now interacting with his fans in the flesh…

00:13:04.320 --> 00:13:09.120
and to them, his persona and the&nbsp;
real person were not lining up.

00:13:09.120 --> 00:13:11.920
They mistook persona for person

00:13:11.920 --> 00:13:18.080
and in so doing became part of an endless,
frustrating cycle between the writer and his fans.

00:13:19.440 --> 00:13:25.040
In short, as Shields put it: “He had made
choices, consciously or unconsciously

00:13:25.040 --> 00:13:28.400
that had created multiple and even contradictory
identities.”

00:13:29.200 --> 00:13:34.080
On a more personal note, Vonnegut’s nephew,
Jim Adams, puts it thusly:

00:13:34.080 --> 00:13:39.120
“There was a definite disconnect between
the kind of guy you would imagine Vonnegut must be

00:13:39.120 --> 00:13:41.120
from the tone of his books, the kind&nbsp;

00:13:41.120 --> 00:13:44.400
of guy who would say, ‘God&nbsp;
damn it, you got to be kind,’

00:13:44.400 --> 00:13:47.520
and the reality of his behavior on a daily basis.

00:13:47.520 --> 00:13:52.400
He was a complicated, difficult man. .&nbsp;
. . I think he admired the idea of love,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:52.400 --> 00:13:55.040
community, and family from a distance

00:13:55.040 --> 00:13:58.480
but couldn’t deal with the complicated emotional
elements they included.”

00:13:59.760 --> 00:14:03.840
Vonnegut once did something few writers would
never have the stomach to do

00:14:04.400 --> 00:14:06.560
he graded his own novels.

00:14:06.560 --> 00:14:09.360
He gave Slapstick the lowest grade, a D

00:14:09.360 --> 00:14:12.720
while Cat’s Cradle and&nbsp;
Slaughterhouse-Five both got A-pluses.

00:14:13.280 --> 00:14:16.400
Way to go, Cat’s Cradle and&nbsp;
Slaughterhouse-Five! Good for you!

00:14:17.440 --> 00:14:19.120
The rest were somewhere in the middle.

00:14:19.680 --> 00:14:21.920
But before he graded them, he emphasized that&nbsp;

00:14:21.920 --> 00:14:25.520
in a world with William&nbsp;
Shakespeare, shout out to him

00:14:25.520 --> 00:14:28.000
“the grades I hand out to myself do not
place me

00:14:28.000 --> 00:14:29.440
in literary history.

00:14:29.440 --> 00:14:32.320
I am comparing myself with myself.”

00:14:33.200 --> 00:14:37.200
Throughout his life and career, Vonnegut’s
sense of self was ever-evolving

00:14:37.200 --> 00:14:40.400
and always at odds with the&nbsp;
blurred line between man and myth.

00:14:40.960 --> 00:14:44.160
It was a myth he both embraced and resisted.

00:14:44.160 --> 00:14:48.720
A myth that even he himself got lost in like
a funhouse of mirrors.

00:14:48.720 --> 00:14:51.920
In his later years, he told fellow writer
Martin Amis:

00:14:51.920 --> 00:14:55.840
“I have to keep reminding myself that I
wrote those early books.

00:14:55.840 --> 00:14:58.160
I wrote that . . . . I wrote that.

00:14:58.160 --> 00:15:01.760
The only way I can regain credit for my early
work is

00:15:01.760 --> 00:15:02.400
to die.”

00:15:03.360 --> 00:15:06.480
Vonnegut did die, but the truth lives on in
his fiction.

00:15:07.360 --> 00:15:11.840
More or less.

